# vLLM's rows: one a model the server can serve, each the model and its server flags, named by
# the pin (VLLM_ROWS in vllm.env). The pin names each flavor's default row, VLLM_MODEL_CUDA's on
# the GB10s and VLLM_MODEL_ROCM's on the Framework; `VLLM_MODEL=<row id> host/engines vllm update`
# serves another for one run (docs/engines/vllm/models.md, "Changing the model"). The rows are one
# set for both flavors, though not every row serves on both: NVFP4 is CUDA's alone.
#
# A row is its id in brackets, then the model's Hugging Face repository, then its settings and its
# server flags, over as many lines as read well. vllm.sh puts --host and --port after the model, so
# a row never names them. An id is the repository's name without its organization, with a suffix
# for a second row of the same model; each is unique. No flag or value holds a space or a #, since
# the served command is compared joined with spaces and # starts a comment.
#
# A row's settings are the suite's, which vllm serve never sees (vllm.sh, vllm_row), each a word
# <key>=<value>: container=<name>, the container it serves from, vllm when it names none;
# port=<port>, the host port it publishes on the tailnet address and listens on, 8000 when it
# names none; and image=<repository>, an image the host builds, served in place of the flavor's
# official one at the same tag (vllm_image), and never pulled. Rows with distinct containers and
# ports serve side by side on one host, one process each; rows that share a container replace each
# other in it.
#
# Each row reserves the fraction of the GPU's memory its --gpu-memory-utilization names, which
# leaves the rest to the host it shares it with. A row that serves alone on its host reserves 0.80.
# Rows that serve together on one host each reserve a share, and their fractions' sum is what vLLM
# takes of the host, so each set of them is sized for its host. A row that sizes its KV cache in
# bytes (--kv-cache-memory-bytes) keeps its fraction, and vLLM takes the bytes in place of what the
# fraction would leave the cache. Every row serves sequences of 8192 tokens, as each of llama.cpp's
# slots, but the agent rows, which serve the agent's 131,072. The model's own generation config sets
# its sampling (vLLM's default, --generation-config auto). Gemma 4's rows hold 4 sequences,
# mirroring llama.cpp's 4 slots, but the capability rows, which hold 16; keep a BF16 KV cache, since
# the NVFP4 checkpoints declare a static FP8 one but ship no calibrated k/v scales, so vLLM would
# quantize it with a scale of 1.0, and every Gemma 4 row matches them; and use Gemma 4's own
# reasoning and tool-call parsers. Their vision encoder loads (no --language-model-only), and E4B's
# and 12B's audio encoder too: vLLM allows every modality the model has without
# --limit-mm-per-prompt.

# The cuda flavor's default: Gemma 4 26B-A4B, MoE, NVIDIA's NVFP4. The anchors A' and B', and
# vision.
[gemma-4-26B-A4B-NVFP4]
nvidia/Gemma-4-26B-A4B-NVFP4
--max-num-seqs 4 --max-model-len 8192 --gpu-memory-utilization 0.80
--kv-cache-dtype bfloat16
--reasoning-parser gemma4 --enable-auto-tool-choice --tool-call-parser gemma4

# The FlashInfer diagnostic: the pin's row on FlashInfer attention, text-only. Without a backend
# named, vLLM forces Triton for Gemma 4's mixed head sizes on the GB10, where FA4 isn't available;
# --language-model-only leaves the vision encoder out, so only the text model's attention runs.
[gemma-4-26B-A4B-NVFP4-flashinfer]
nvidia/Gemma-4-26B-A4B-NVFP4
--max-num-seqs 4 --max-model-len 8192 --gpu-memory-utilization 0.80
--kv-cache-dtype bfloat16
--reasoning-parser gemma4 --enable-auto-tool-choice --tool-call-parser gemma4
--attention-backend FLASHINFER --language-model-only

# The Marlin MoE diagnostic: the pin's row on Marlin's kernels for the NVFP4 experts. Without a
# backend named, vLLM v0.32.0 chooses FLASHINFER_CUTLASS for them on the GB10's SM121, out of the
# backends its journal lists, MARLIN among them; Marlin is the fallback in vLLM's own list, a
# weight-only kernel that runs the activations in 16 bits. The row asks whether the MoE kernel
# explains vLLM's slow NVFP4 decode, so only the MoE backend changes.
[gemma-4-26B-A4B-NVFP4-marlin]
nvidia/Gemma-4-26B-A4B-NVFP4
--max-num-seqs 4 --max-model-len 8192 --gpu-memory-utilization 0.80
--kv-cache-dtype bfloat16
--reasoning-parser gemma4 --enable-auto-tool-choice --tool-call-parser gemma4
--moe-backend marlin

# Gemma 4 26B-A4B, MoE, Google's BF16, with its vision encoder: the MoE shape in a format ROCm
# serves, where the NVFP4 rows are CUDA's alone. The anchors and vision on the Framework.
[gemma-4-26B-A4B-it]
google/gemma-4-26B-A4B-it
--max-num-seqs 4 --max-model-len 8192 --gpu-memory-utilization 0.80
--kv-cache-dtype bfloat16
--reasoning-parser gemma4 --enable-auto-tool-choice --tool-call-parser gemma4

# Gemma 4 E4B, dense small, BF16.
[gemma-4-E4B-it]
google/gemma-4-E4B-it
--max-num-seqs 4 --max-model-len 8192 --gpu-memory-utilization 0.80
--kv-cache-dtype bfloat16
--reasoning-parser gemma4 --enable-auto-tool-choice --tool-call-parser gemma4

# Gemma 4 E4B, Google's quantization-aware trained w4a16: the quantization pair beside BF16.
[gemma-4-E4B-it-qat-w4a16-ct]
google/gemma-4-E4B-it-qat-w4a16-ct
--max-num-seqs 4 --max-model-len 8192 --gpu-memory-utilization 0.80
--kv-cache-dtype bfloat16
--reasoning-parser gemma4 --enable-auto-tool-choice --tool-call-parser gemma4

# Gemma 4 12B, dense mid, Google's QAT w4a16.
[gemma-4-12B-it-qat-w4a16-ct]
google/gemma-4-12B-it-qat-w4a16-ct
--max-num-seqs 4 --max-model-len 8192 --gpu-memory-utilization 0.80
--kv-cache-dtype bfloat16
--reasoning-parser gemma4 --enable-auto-tool-choice --tool-call-parser gemma4

# Gemma 4 31B, dense large, NVIDIA's NVFP4; it declares the static FP8 KV cache too.
[Gemma-4-31B-IT-NVFP4]
nvidia/Gemma-4-31B-IT-NVFP4
--max-num-seqs 4 --max-model-len 8192 --gpu-memory-utilization 0.80
--kv-cache-dtype bfloat16
--reasoning-parser gemma4 --enable-auto-tool-choice --tool-call-parser gemma4

# Gemma 4 31B, dense large, Google's QAT w4a16: the dense large shape in a format ROCm serves.
[gemma-4-31B-it-qat-w4a16-ct]
google/gemma-4-31B-it-qat-w4a16-ct
--max-num-seqs 4 --max-model-len 8192 --gpu-memory-utilization 0.80
--kv-cache-dtype bfloat16
--reasoning-parser gemma4 --enable-auto-tool-choice --tool-call-parser gemma4

# IBM's granite-embedding-english-r2, BF16, served for /v1/embeddings only: vLLM's pooling runner,
# which its architecture selects anyway, named so the row says what it serves. A ModernBERT
# encoder with heads of 64, whose encoder-only attention runs on FLASH_ATTN on the GB10's SM121.
# 16 sequences, one a stream of the embedding cell's 16. No parsers: it generates nothing.
[granite-embedding-english-r2]
ibm-granite/granite-embedding-english-r2
--runner pooling
--max-num-seqs 16 --max-model-len 8192 --gpu-memory-utilization 0.80

# OpenAI's gpt-oss-120b in its native MXFP4, the rocm flavor's default, at 16 sequences,
# mirroring llama.cpp's 16-slot section: its anchor-load cells and the concurrency sweep run on
# this one start. Its KV cache stays the checkpoint's (BF16), and its parsers are gpt-oss's:
# openai_gptoss for the reasoning, which vLLM would choose for gpt-oss anyway, and openai for the
# tool calls.
[gpt-oss-120b]
openai/gpt-oss-120b
--max-num-seqs 16 --max-model-len 8192 --gpu-memory-utilization 0.80
--reasoning-parser openai_gptoss --enable-auto-tool-choice --tool-call-parser openai

# gpt-oss-120b for the agent (spike-model-layout's agent-vllm task, docs/findings/agent-vllm.md
# there): the gpt-oss-120b row at the agent's sessions, 131,072 tokens each. Its KV cache stays
# the checkpoint's (BF16), sized by the 0.80, and no attention or MoE backend is named, so vLLM
# chooses its own. It holds 8 at once, the highest level of the agent sweep at which it met the
# agent's floor (an ITL median of at most 100 ms; agent-vllm F, "Meets the floor through"), set by
# spike-model-layout's vllm-layout task: past 8 a session queues rather than slowing the others.
[gpt-oss-120b-agent]
openai/gpt-oss-120b
--max-num-seqs 8 --max-model-len 131072 --gpu-memory-utilization 0.80
--reasoning-parser openai_gptoss --enable-auto-tool-choice --tool-call-parser openai

# The agent row on an FP8 KV cache (fp8, which vLLM reads as e4m3 on CUDA), sized in bytes to hold
# llama.cpp's agent-only pool of 1,179,648 tokens: 32993476982 bytes, about 30.73 GiB. The bytes
# size the cache in place of the 0.80, which the row keeps for the rest. No backend is named, so
# the start log records the attention backend vLLM chooses for gpt-oss's sinks on an FP8 cache.
# It holds 12 at once, the highest level at which it met the agent's floor (agent-vllm F), set by
# vllm-layout as the BF16 row's cap is.
[gpt-oss-120b-agent-fp8]
openai/gpt-oss-120b
--max-num-seqs 12 --max-model-len 131072 --gpu-memory-utilization 0.80
--kv-cache-dtype fp8 --kv-cache-memory-bytes 32993476982
--reasoning-parser openai_gptoss --enable-auto-tool-choice --tool-call-parser openai

# The capability rows (spike-model-layout's capability-vllm task, docs/findings/capability-vllm.md
# there), placed by its vllm-layout task: vision on 8000, audio on 8001, embedding on 8002, each in
# its own container on its own port at its own fraction. Each serves sequences of 8192 tokens, and
# names no attention or MoE backend, so vLLM chooses its own and the start log records it.
#
# vllm-layout keeps the purposes apart by placement. Beside each other and granite on one GPU,
# vision and audio missed their floors on both hosts while granite met its own, with memory far
# from gb10-2's floor (capability-vllm H, I, N.1); so gb10-2 serves vision and audio alone, with no
# embedding row, 0.60 in all of its 121.63 GiB, and the Framework serves granite alone. Its other
# rows stay out of its standing set, which the rows file has no list for, so their comments say it:
# the 12B and E4B rows are a manual failover for gb10-2's two, staged and stopped, and the BF16
# 26B-A4B row is vllm-layout's comparison and accuracy reference, started only for its checks. The
# 12B and the BF16 26B-A4B share the vllm-vision container, so either replaces the other in it.

# gb10-2's vision: Gemma 4 26B-A4B in NVIDIA's NVFP4, 0.40, beside gb10-2's audio row alone.
[gemma-4-26B-A4B-NVFP4-vision-gb10]
nvidia/Gemma-4-26B-A4B-NVFP4
container=vllm-vision port=8000
--max-num-seqs 16 --max-model-len 8192 --gpu-memory-utilization 0.40
--kv-cache-dtype bfloat16
--reasoning-parser gemma4 --enable-auto-tool-choice --tool-call-parser gemma4

# gb10-2's audio: Gemma 4 E4B, BF16, 0.20, on the official image, beside gb10-2's vision row alone.
[gemma-4-E4B-it-audio-gb10]
google/gemma-4-E4B-it
container=vllm-audio port=8001
--max-num-seqs 16 --max-model-len 8192 --gpu-memory-utilization 0.20
--kv-cache-dtype bfloat16
--reasoning-parser gemma4 --enable-auto-tool-choice --tool-call-parser gemma4

# The Framework's vision failover: Gemma 4 12B in Google's quantization-aware trained w4a16
# (compressed-tensors, Apache 2.0, google/gemma-4-12B-it-qat-w4a16-ct, 9.56 GiB), 0.20, in place of
# gb10-2's vision row when it is down; staged and stopped. vllm-layout chose it as the lighter
# Gemma 4 vision model for the Framework's ROCm engine: its three rows at 0.60 (the BF16 26B-A4B),
# 0.18 and 0.03 took its system memory to 0.76 GiB above its floor (capability-vllm K, N.2).
[gemma-4-12B-it-qat-w4a16-ct-vision-framework]
google/gemma-4-12B-it-qat-w4a16-ct
container=vllm-vision port=8000
--max-num-seqs 16 --max-model-len 8192 --gpu-memory-utilization 0.20
--kv-cache-dtype bfloat16
--reasoning-parser gemma4 --enable-auto-tool-choice --tool-call-parser gemma4

# The Framework's BF16 reference: Gemma 4 26B-A4B in Google's BF16, the only 26B-A4B format vLLM
# has served on gfx1151, 0.60. Not a standing row: vllm-layout starts it beside granite for the
# Framework's vision sweep against the 12B and as the BF16 reference its accuracy checks score
# NVFP4 and the 12B against, then removes its weights from the store.
[gemma-4-26B-A4B-it-vision-framework]
google/gemma-4-26B-A4B-it
container=vllm-vision port=8000
--max-num-seqs 16 --max-model-len 8192 --gpu-memory-utilization 0.60
--kv-cache-dtype bfloat16
--reasoning-parser gemma4 --enable-auto-tool-choice --tool-call-parser gemma4

# The Framework's audio failover: Gemma 4 E4B, BF16, 0.18, from the audio image (vllm-rocm-audio),
# since the official ROCm image ships none of the audio extra's decoders, and every audio request
# on it returns 500. The host builds the image, at the tag of the ROCm build it serves. In place of
# gb10-2's audio row when it is down; staged and stopped.
[gemma-4-E4B-it-audio-framework]
google/gemma-4-E4B-it
container=vllm-audio port=8001 image=spike-model-engines/vllm-openai-rocm-audio
--max-num-seqs 16 --max-model-len 8192 --gpu-memory-utilization 0.18
--kv-cache-dtype bfloat16
--reasoning-parser gemma4 --enable-auto-tool-choice --tool-call-parser gemma4

# The Framework's embedding, its standing row: granite-embedding-english-r2, on vLLM's pooling
# runner, 0.03. 128 sequences and 65,536 batched tokens a step: vLLM's default of 8,192 tokens
# batches only 16 of the embedding cells' 512-token inputs a step (capability-vllm G), and a
# document pipeline sends more; the sweep vllm-layout runs to 128 streams reads the knee.
[granite-embedding-english-r2-framework]
ibm-granite/granite-embedding-english-r2
container=vllm-embed port=8002
--runner pooling
--max-num-seqs 128 --max-model-len 8192 --gpu-memory-utilization 0.03
--max-num-batched-tokens 65536
